<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Burgh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as de Burgh family, Burkes, Burke family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Burgh was an Anglo-Norman family that acquired vast lands in Ireland after the invasion of the 12th century. William de Burgh founded the line, and his descendants became lords of Connacht and earls of Ulster. In the 14th century its Connacht branches adopted Gaelic ways as the Burkes, or Mac William, while pushing Irish lords such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oflaherty">O'Flahertys</a> westwards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-burgh.jpg" alt="House of Burgh" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_House_of_de_Burgh.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Clanricarde <b>Burkes</b> adopted Irish law, language and customs, intermarrying with Gaelic families while competing with the O'Briens and with Galway town.</blockquote><a href="/state/clanricarde">Clanricarde</a></li><li><blockquote>Driven westwards by the Anglo-Norman <b>de Burghs</b> in the 13th century, they established themselves in Iar Connacht, the land west of Lough Corrib including Connemara.</blockquote><a href="/state/iar-connacht">Iar Connacht</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mayo <b>Burkes</b> lived as Gaelic lords, electing their chief by tanistry, employing gallowglasses and poets, and allying with or fighting the O'Donnells, O'Malleys and the Clanricarde Burkes.</blockquote><a href="/state/mac-william-iochtar">Mac William Iochtar</a></li></ul></section>
